European Union foreign ministers have  welcomed the landmark agreement reached  between Serbia and Kosovo which could  pave the way toward EU membership at the  General Affairs Council in Luxembourg  The ministers received reports on the  two countries from the European  Commission just as it recommended that  Serbia be allowed to begin EU membership  talks and Kosovo started stabilisation  and association agreement process there  was a very strong sense that last  Friday's agreement was indeed historic  was a game-changer was a very positive  day for both Serbia and Kosovo and very  many tributes were paid to the two prime  ministers for the political courage that  they showed and reaching that agreement  Friday's agreements or Serbia  relinquished control over the northern  part of Kosovo where tensions of run  high between ethnic Serbs and the ethnic  Albanian majority in Kosovo Serbia still  refuses to recognize the independence of  Kosovo which unilaterally broke away  with Western support in 2008 some  ministers warned of the challenges ahead  in implementing the agreement  implementation will not be entirely see  but i think the Rubicon has been passed  there is no way back so wide wheel  struggled with the details of the  implementation for quite some time to  come the Rubicon has been paused during  their meeting the ministers were also  informed about the state of negotiations  over the e u--'s 2014-2020 budget with  the european parliament and asked for  their guidance on the road ahead the  Irish presidency expressed hope that a  deal will be clinched in time for the  new budget programs to begin in January
 

